# The resolver watchdog: put the machine's own resolvers back when the local one
# has stopped answering, unattended, once a window of failures has been reached.
#
# ## This is the only unit in the package that changes the machine by itself
#
# Every other service here reads, measures and publishes. This one runs
# `emergency-rollback`, which rewrites a NetworkManager connection, on a timer,
# with nobody watching. The operator who owns the machine decided that: automatic
# rollback is the DEFAULT and it must be possible to switch it off, and
# /etc/mosdns/watchdog.yaml is the switch.
#
# So this unit's job is to be as narrow as the decision allows, and the three
# facts below are the whole of it.
#
# ## 1. It acts only on a WINDOW, and the window is a setting
#
# One failed probe is not evidence: a slow upstream, a cold boot before NTP has
# synchronised and a loaded machine each produce one. With automatic rollback as
# the default, a single-probe trigger is a mechanism that can tear down a working
# configuration while somebody is using it, and a self-inflicted outage is worse
# than a false alarm. So it acts on the Nth consecutive failure or once M minutes
# have passed since the first, and both numbers are in
# /etc/mosdns/watchdog.yaml because the false-positive rate has never been
# measured and a number nobody measured does not get baked in.
#
# ## 2. It runs the OPERATOR'S command, not one of its own
#
# `mosdns-cdnctl emergency-rollback` is exactly what a person types. The watchdog
# execs that argument array and reports the status it comes back with. A watchdog
# with its own rollback path would be a second implementation of the most
# deliberate code in this package, and the two would disagree eventually.
#
# That verb refuses to run as a non-root uid, which is the only reason this unit
# runs as root where every other service in the package runs as its own
# unprivileged identity. It grants no capability: the action talks to
# NetworkManager over D-Bus, and the daemon on the other end does the writing.
#
# ## 3. It is a TIMER, so it inherits the packaged hardening
#
# The same sandbox as mosdns-cdn-health.service, the same bounding set, the same
# umask, and one writable directory: /run/mosdns/watchdog, for the failure record
# at /run/mosdns/watchdog/watchdog.json and nothing else. It is that subdirectory
# and not /run/mosdns, which is group-writable by design, and a per-unit subset
# would be a privilege decision nobody can review at a glance.
#
# ## What it does NOT claim
#
# A successful rollback restores the machine's own DHCP-provided resolvers, so
# basic name resolution works again. **Foreign-name resolution through DNSCrypt
# does not work afterwards**, and that is the condition this package was
# installed to fix. The success message says both, every time, because a message
# that said only the first would be true and useless at 3am.
#
# And the ordering: this unit is `After=` NetworkManager because it runs `nmcli`
# against it, and carries none of Requires=, BindsTo=, PartOf=, PropagatesStopTo=
# or Upholds=. That is measured, not preferred: `Requires=` on a unit a service
# only talks to through D-Bus is a cycle, and NetworkManager's own restart would
# take this unit down with it.
#
# VERSION FLOOR: systemd 249, the oldest supported release (Ubuntu 22.04). The
# newest directive in this file is ProtectProc= and ProcSubset=, both 247.

[Unit]
Description=Resolver watchdog: restore the machine's own resolvers when the local one stops answering
Documentation=man:mosdns-router(8)
After=network.target NetworkManager.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=root
Group=root
ExecStart=/usr/lib/mosdns-router/mosdns_installer.py watchdog
# The probe is one 2-second query. The action is not: it reactivates a
# NetworkManager connection, which re-runs DHCP, and it gets its own budget
# inside the verb -- one that is DERIVED rather than chosen, because the previous
# one (150s) was smaller than the work it was supposed to bound.
#
# The work is the action's GRANDCHILD: `mosdns-cdnctl emergency-rollback` is a
# launcher that execs the installer's own `emergency-rollback` verb, and that verb
# issues up to 9 commands (four property reads, three property writes, one
# `nmcli connection up`, one `resolvectl dns`) on a runner with the package's
# default 30-second per-command budget. 9 x 30 + a 2s probe + 30s of slack = 302s,
# which is the budget the verb gives it. THIS is above that, so the watchdog
# prints its own report rather than being killed part way through a restore. A
# rollback killed mid-restore is the worst thing this package can do unattended.
#
# The number below is not decorative and the gate over it is not a comparison of
# two constants: `installer/tests/test_watchdog.py` measures how many commands
# the rollback actually issues, re-derives 302 from that measurement and the
# per-command budget, and requires this to be above the result. A sixth command
# or a longer per-command timeout makes that gate red here.
TimeoutStartSec=330
# No SuccessExitStatus. Every non-zero exit is something an operator has to read:
# 1 is this program's own refusal (the resolver is not answering and the window
# has not been reached, the setting could not be read, the record could not be
# written, or another operation holds the control lock), and anything else is the
# ACTION's own status, passed through unchanged so a caller can tell a rollback
# that did not finish from one that changed nothing.
#
# **TWO PATHS EXIT 0 WITHOUT ACTING**, and claiming otherwise would be false:
# the switch is off, which is an operator's decision and not a fault; and there
# is no verdict at all -- the operator's force-ECH list makes the router answer
# the probe name itself, so a probe whose answer is meaningless was not counted.
# Neither is a run in which the watchdog "reported success about the machine",
# because neither one had a verdict to report. Both print what they found and
# both say plainly that nothing was changed.
CapabilityBoundingSet=
# ## 4. It stands down while something else is changing this machine
#
# On an upgrade this timer is already enabled, and the install transaction then
# try-restarts the two units that serve 127.0.0.1:53. While they restart, the
# local resolver is not answering BY CONSTRUCTION -- the transaction creates that
# failure deliberately, for up to a minute a unit -- and a watchdog that read it
# as a fault would fire an emergency rollback underneath a running install. At
# the shipped defaults three failed probes are two minutes, so the threshold is
# comfortably inside that window.
#
# So this run takes the package's shared control lock,
# /var/lib/mosdns/runtime/control.lock, after probing and before it believes
# anything: the same lock the optimizer, the health check and `update-lists`
# take, and the one the transaction now holds across the window in which it
# moves this machine's DNS. A run that cannot take it counts nothing and acts on
# nothing, and says which lock and who holds it. That is the same protection the
# `OnBootSec=3min` above gives at boot, through the mechanism this package
# already has rather than a fourth one.
#
# The lock is taken WITHOUT waiting and released before the action runs: this is
# a timer, and blocking on it would mean sitting for the length of a transaction
# and then acting anyway.
#
# AND IT IS OPENED READ-ONLY, WITHOUT O_CREAT, which is not a detail. The mount
# namespace above makes /var/lib/mosdns/runtime read-only, so creating the lock
# here fails with EROFS -- measured, on a live cell, and the symptom was this
# unit declining to do anything at all on every machine whose optimizer had not
# yet run. Absence is free: every holder creates the lock first, so a lock that
# is not there is a lock nobody holds. That is the same discipline as the DHCP
# bridge's own publication lock.
#
# 0007, inherited from the health check's, and it does NOT describe this record:
# the record is 0600 and its directory is 0700 root:root, because nothing in this
# package but this unit reads it. The umask is the packaged one -- a per-unit
# privilege decision nobody can review at a glance -- and the record's own mode is
# pinned explicitly rather than inherited from it, since a umask can only narrow.
UMask=0007

# The same sandbox the router and the health check get. This verb reads two
# documents under /etc, writes one file under /run, opens one loopback UDP
# socket, and execs a program that talks to NetworkManager over D-Bus. It uses no
# temporary file, no device, no kernel setting, no process list and no namespace.
NoNewPrivileges=true
PrivateTmp=true
PrivateDevices=true
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=true
ProtectKernelTunables=true
ProtectKernelModules=true
ProtectKernelLogs=true
ProtectControlGroups=true
ProtectClock=true
ProtectHostname=true
ProtectProc=invisible
ProcSubset=pid
LockPersonality=true
RestrictNamespaces=true
RestrictRealtime=true
RestrictSUIDSGID=true
SystemCallArchitectures=native

# AF_UNIX for the D-Bus connection to NetworkManager that `emergency-rollback`
# makes, AF_INET for the probe. No interface is enumerated and no netlink socket
# is opened, so this is the health check's list exactly.
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_INET AF_INET6

# Exactly one directory, and it is the one the failure record lives in: its own,
# 0700 root:root subdirectory rather than /run/mosdns itself. /run/mosdns is
# 2770 root:mosdns with a default ACL on purpose, because the DHCP bridge
# publishes there as mosdns-cdn and needs directory w -- and unlink and rename
# are the containing directory's decision, not the record's mode, so a 0600 file
# in there could still be deleted or replaced by that identity. The `-` prefix is
# what makes a missing path tolerated: /run is a tmpfs, so this directory is gone
# after a reboot and an unprefixed entry naming it would fail this unit's
# mount-namespace setup on a host that has not created it yet -- and the retry that
# follows would be the machine's only automatic DNS protection coming up and going
# down. The tmpfiles entry and `postinst` both create it for the same reason.
ReadWritePaths=-/run/mosdns/watchdog
